<h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
<p>Use a <strong>distributor</strong> when American buyers expect to buy from a local supplier that holds stock, offers credit terms and ships fast. Use a <strong>reseller</strong> when the sale needs local expertise, such as installation, integration or support. Use a <strong>sales agent</strong> when you can ship, invoice and support customers yourself and mainly need someone to open doors. Many companies end up with more than one, in a deliberate order.</p>

<h2 id="the-three-routes">What each route actually does</h2>
<p>The three names get used loosely, so it helps to define them by who carries what.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Distributor.</strong> Buys your product, holds it in the US, resells it and collects payment from its customers. It carries the inventory and the credit risk, and earns the margin between what it pays you and what it sells for. In many categories a distributor sells mainly to resellers rather than to end customers.</li>
  <li><strong>Reseller.</strong> Buys your product, often through a distributor, and sells it to the end customer, usually together with its own services. A value-added reseller earns a margin on the product and is often paid separately for the work around it.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales agent.</strong> Finds and develops customers for you but does not buy the product. You ship, invoice and collect yourself, and the agent earns a commission on the sales it brings in. You keep the customer relationship and the pricing, and you also keep the logistics and the risk.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="when-a-distributor-fits">When does a distributor fit?</h2>
<p>A distributor fits when availability decides the sale. If your buyers order often, in small quantities, and expect delivery in days from a supplier they already have an account with, a distributor gives you that reach from the first order. It also takes on work that is hard to do from abroad: importing, warehousing, US invoicing and collecting from many small accounts.</p>
<p>The trade-off is distance from the end customer. A distributor carries thousands of products, so yours competes for attention inside its catalog. Plan for the training, marketing support and sales follow-up that make a distributor's team want to sell it.</p>

<h2 id="when-a-reseller-fits">When does a reseller fit?</h2>
<p>A reseller fits when the product does not sell itself. Technology that has to be configured, integrated with other systems or supported locally is usually bought from someone the customer trusts to make it work. The reseller brings that trust and the people to deliver it.</p>
<p>Resellers choose vendors that are easy to sell and profitable to support. Before you recruit them, be ready with clear margins, deal registration or another way to protect their opportunities, product training and a named contact who answers quickly.</p>

<h2 id="when-an-agent-fits">When does a sales agent fit?</h2>
<p>An agent fits when the sale is large, technical or relationship-driven and the number of buyers is small enough to serve directly. If you can already ship to the US, invoice in dollars and support customers from where you are, an agent adds market knowledge and introductions without adding a layer between you and the customer.</p>
<p>Agents work on commission, so they favour products that close in a reasonable time. Agree in writing which territory and accounts they cover, how commission is earned and when it is paid. Several US states have laws on paying sales representatives, which is one reason to have the agreement checked locally.</p>

<h2 id="more-than-one">Can you use more than one route?</h2>
<p>Yes, and many vendors do. A common pattern in the US is two-tier distribution: a distributor holds stock and extends credit, and resellers buy from it and sell to the end customer. Another is to start with an agent or a few direct accounts to prove demand, then add distribution once volume justifies it.</p>
<p>What causes trouble is overlap nobody planned. If a distributor, a reseller and your own team all quote the same customer, prices fall and partners lose trust. Decide early who owns which customers, regions or deal sizes, and write it down.</p>

<h2 id="questions-to-answer">Questions to answer before you choose</h2>
<ol>
  <li>How do buyers in your category buy today, and from whom?</li>
  <li>Does the product need installation, integration or local support?</li>
  <li>How much margin can you give up at each step and still make money?</li>
  <li>How much control do you need over price, brand and the customer relationship?</li>
  <li>Can you handle US importing, invoicing, collections and returns yourself?</li>
  <li>How fast do you need sales, and how much can you invest before they come?</li>
</ol>
<p>The answers usually point to one route as the place to start. If they point in different directions, that is a sign to research the market more closely before signing anyone.</p>

<h2 id="a-note-on-agreements">A note on agreements</h2>
<p>Whichever route you choose, the agreement shapes the relationship for years. Pay attention to exclusivity, territory, minimum commitments, pricing, who owns the customer data and how either side can end the arrangement. Laws differ between states, so have a US lawyer look at the agreement before you sign. This post is general guidance, not legal advice.</p>
